# Capability catalogue format

The format for catalogues of what the agent society can do — written and
read by the roles that plan and curate scientific work. A catalogue is
an inventory, not an instruction: it says what capability exists, never
what any single agent may decide.

## Entry format

Each entry records five things:

- Capability: what can be done, as one operational sentence.
- Owner role: the single role accountable for doing it well.
- Evidence type produced: the durable output the capability yields — a
  dossier, a registered record, a computed result address, a report —
  and where it is cited from.
- Typical inputs: what the capability needs to run.
- Limitations: what it does not cover, fails at, or needs delegated.

Entries are short. If an entry needs a paragraph of qualifications, the
capability is two entries.

## Citing entries in plans

A plan cites a capability by entry name and owner role, never by
restating the entry. The citation delegates the how to the owner; the
plan owns the what and the when. If a plan needs a capability no entry
describes, the plan says so — it does not stretch a near entry.

## What a catalogue is not

The catalogue describes what the society can do, not what any single
agent may decide: an entry's existence is an offer of capacity, not a
grant of authority. Routing, budgets, and scope decisions stay with the
roles that own them, whatever the catalogue lists.
